WHAT THIS BUILD CHANGES (1.9.15 -> 1.9.16), all of it about the su-name-gate patch:
  1. The 7 route's window witness moved BEFORE the .text write.  `esc_sugate7` is now four entry
     points that only write -- `open` (plant the 1 GB alias in the empty pgd slot, move
     /proc/sys/kernel/modprobe to "/zz" through it), `restore`, `patch`, `abort` -- and the READS are
     done by the caller over the daemon's root channel.  If either witness fails, `abort` puts the
     string back at the REAL VA and not one byte of .text is written.  1.9.15 patched anyway when its
     own in-process read failed, which is the rule "no read-back, no write" broken (ledger 676).
     Cold-boot reading from the shipped chain, no adb input:
        sugate7 open: rc=0   alias entry 0xffffffc001f4cff8 rc=1   witness write 0xffffffffc1a499b8 rc=1
        window PROVEN through the alias: modprobe_path "/sbin/modprobe" -> "/zz"
        sugate7 restore: rc=0   (writes the full 64-byte modprobe_path, zero-padded)
        restore witnessed: "/sbin/modprobe" came back through the alias, both directions proven
        sugate7 patch: rc=0   addr 0xffffffffc0323400 (= 0xffffffc000323400 via alias), NOP at 0x323400
        app-exec su: execve allowed   ->  one needle this boot (broot7 attempt 1 rc=0), and that is the budget
  2. `restore`/`abort` write the whole char[64] instead of strlen+1: "/zz" truncates the printed path
     but leaves the tail bytes, so restoring only the head can leave a stale tail.
  3. The acceptance harness's grants barrier was blind and is now not: `adb shell cat` on app-private
     data is Permission denied, so the old `grants_until` always read empty, spun its timeout and let
     the verdicts pass on timing alone.  It now reads through the root channel with a `GRANTS_EOF`
     sentinel (so "channel did not answer" is separate from "list is empty"), and a barrier that never
     confirmed turns its criterion into REFUSED instead of a footnote next to a PASS.
  4. Three stale statements corrected where they were written (Chain.openSuName7's comment, this
     release's predecessor STATUS, project memory): "the gate's switch has not been located on
     3.18.31", "no kernel address is written here", "the app cannot read /proc/sys/kernel/modprobe".
     The last one was never a reading -- the shipped chain's own log shows the in-process read working.

KNOWN DEFECT IN THIS PACKAGE (measured, not hidden):
  * At boot, `selfGrant` logs `self-grant uid=10151 added saved=false` -- the list read returns empty
    and the write then fails at unlink, so the allow list is NOT damaged.  Long-standing: the same line
    appears on earlier boots (ledger 5062/5181 sections).  What it means for THIS build is worse than a
    log wart: 1.9.16 treats "could not read" as "empty list", so the owner's third-party entries
    (Shizuku 10133, snub 10135) survive the boot self-grant only because the unlink happens to fail.
    1.9.17 (vc38, built and installed, awaiting its own boot reading) separates the two --
    `readGrantsOrNull` returns null for a failed read -- and refuses to rewrite with the errno logged.
  * MECHANISM OF THE BOOT-TIME FAILURE, NAMED THE SAME DAY (ledger 677, single-factor A/B across two
    boots): the inode the acceptance harness left behind was made by root (`grep > x.t; mv x.t list`),
    and inside app data that inode arrives WITHOUT the app's MLS categories -- `u0_a151:root` +
    `app_data_file:s0` instead of `u0_a151:u0_a151` + `...:s0:c512,c768`.  With enforcing up, that
    appdomain can neither read the file nor unlink it, which is exactly `added saved=false`.  Restoring
    the shape (chown uid AND group, then `restorecon`) made the next boot's identical chain log
    `self-grant uid=10151 resealed saved=true list=[10151, 10133, 10135] why=-`.
    A mid-boot `saved=true` seen from the app's own 撤销全部 button does NOT contradict this: it was
    tapped after broot7 had planted the permissive-map exemption, i.e. inside a window we opened
    ourselves.  Judge the shape only from the un-escalated side.
  * The harness that created the bad shape is fixed in the same commit: `drop_demo` now rewrites the
    list in place (`cat >`, inode/owner/label untouched) and `seal_grants` restores the group and runs
    `restorecon`.

BOUNDS AND LIMITS OF THIS DELIVERY
  * Memory-only.  Bootloader never unlocked, no partition written; `adb reboot` restores the vendor
    name gate, drops /sbin/su and the daemon, and the harness's G criterion measures exactly that.
  * One kernel needle per boot at most; the boot trigger does not re-fire the needle on a boot that is
    already served.
  * `job schedule rc=1` on this ROM: JobScheduler refuses the re-check job, so if relayd dies it is not
    re-armed by the scheduler -- re-arming happens when the app process starts again or when 提权 is
    tapped.  Recorded as a product boundary, not a defect to fix in this route.
  * The 6.0.1 route (`esc_sugate`) still keeps the older shape: when its own enforce read is not
    available it patches and relies on the exec witness (clobber.c:898).  Unchanged in this build
    because this phone is running 7 and that branch cannot be witnessed here.
  * Byte policy of this package: `assets/broot7` is byte-identical to 1.9.15's (cmp), the binary that
    has actually booted and escalated; `classes.dex` and `lib/arm64-v8a/libesc.so` are byte-identical
    to the artifact measured on the boot above (cmp).  A freshly relinked broot7 (+520 B, five extra
    `sugate7*` symbols broot7 never calls) was deliberately NOT shipped.

